 "name": "Rain",
 "_note": "Rain as four layers, not one. (1) 'rain-fall' is the body of the shower: a 72 x 18 x 72 m box that follows the camera in x and z (ADR-520 volumeFollow) with wrapping on, so 26 k drops are always the 26 k drops that are in shot and none of them is two hundred metres behind the lens. Wrapping is what makes it STEADY: without it every drop has to be born and to die inside the frame, and a drop that fades in is the single most obvious tell of computer rain. (2) 'rain-near' is eleven hundred large, soft, nearly transparent drops in a 7 m box riding with the camera -- the out-of-focus foreground layer a real lens gives you, and the reason the shower has depth instead of being one wall of streaks. (3) 'rain-impacts' spawns drops 40 cm above the ground and lets ADR-520's splash response turn each one into an expanding ground ring where it lands; the ripples are therefore correlated in time with a fall, not sprinkled on. (4) 'rain-mist' is the air the rain has already wetted. dragSizeBias 0.85 is what stops the fall reading as a machine: the drops drawn large by sizeSkew are also drawn fast, so the field has a spread of speeds that is correlated with a spread of sizes the way it is in the world.",
